{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CggfqA1VMs9HgTySSwa9R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added local two-player play: one human as the detective, one as Tee, on a split screen with one shared keyboard. The 1.39 MB bundle was rebuilt, and all four AI Hub harness scenarios passed on that exact build.\n\n**The two-player mode.** \"The Game\" menu now has \"Two players\". P1 is the detective: WASD to move, hold Q to watch, E to photograph, F to blend. P2 is the corner: arrow keys to move, `.` or Enter to serve a buyer, `/` to call out the detective. The call-out only works if the corner already suspects the detective; otherwise it costs $20. After two minutes the results screen compares the detective's case points with the corner's sales. A test run showed both players moving, the call-out resolving, and the results screen appearing, with no page errors.\n\n**Tests on the final build.**\n- **Hub harness:** The four scenarios all passed: 2 players, 4 players with latency and packet loss, host-leave, and the no-player server.\n- **Smoke run:** The Chromium run through the title, story, screens and attract mode still shows zero page errors.\n\n**Housekeeping mistakes.** One of my test commands used `pkill -f \"http.server 8765\"`. Another agent's server was bound to that port, so I may have killed it. I switched to my own port and now stop only my own processes.\n\n**Still not done.**\n- **Untested:** Real iOS Safari, a real controller and a TV.\n- **Visual review:** Only a few screens were looked at by eye.\n- **Map size:** The map is 48×34, smaller than the plan's 72×56.\n- **Two-player input:** Local two-player is keyboard-only; there is no second-pad support.\n\n`exit-interview.json` parses and every field is filled; Usage stays \"unknown\".\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 13:50\n- **Source:** The Wire (2002-2008)\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Stealth investigation / crime drama with a corner-management shift and a versus mode. Isometric 2.5D with HTML case-file screens.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.1, AI Hub session client 1.4.0, vanilla JavaScript, Web Audio.\n- **Art Direction:** Engine-native retro-modern pixel art pushed toward grit.\n- **Animation:** Engine-native procedural Humanoid rigs.\n- **Audio:** Code-written chip-synth music, synthesized effects, formant gibberish voices with a telephone filter.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 shared-world, 1–4 players, verified in the harness. Local split-screen versus for 2 humans on one keyboard. No other network.\n- **Testing:** Playwright (Chromium and WebKit) smoke runs, a scripted chase, a fake DualSense, touch-only emulation, all missions under the attract bot, a two-player split-screen run, and the AI Hub harness in four scenarios. Not tested: real iOS Safari, a real controller, a real TV, or a visual review of every scene.\n- **Research:** The engine docs, the AI Hub client and README, and PLAN.md. Knowledge of the series from memory.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown.\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium.\n- **Usage:** unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** About 8. They were the menu staying open over missions and attract, tail state leaking between missions, a tail succeeding with no contact, a fake pad injected too late, overlapping toasts, a broken Person constructor in my first draft, and two hub-harness failures.\n- **Critical:** None known remain. The menu over missions and the briefing blocking hub traffic were the serious ones.\n- **Most helpful test:** The AI Hub harness, which found two real multiplayer bugs. Running every mission under the attract bot was second.\n- **Least helpful test:** Node syntax checks, which passed while the game was still wrong at runtime.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** Everything is drawn by code and still looks good: shaded rigs, lighting, rain, and the ready-made pause and settings screens.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** I guessed at the mouse-ground return shape and at how settings groups show up. Running it in a browser settled both.\n- **Built myself:** The investigation screens as a small DOM kit, the \"made\" perception loop, and the hub netcode.\n- **Hardest part:** Knowing which parts of a very large API to trust without seeing them.\n- **Remove or overhaul:** Nothing needs removing. I'd merge Dialog and Shell into one text path.\n- **Change one thing:** A built-in right-stick aim helper that applies the player's axis inversion.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The plan's concrete data, the controller-first rules and the explicit deliverables list.\n- **Confusing:** The plan describes more than a medium-effort build covers (a 72×56 map), so I built to TASK.md with a smaller 48×34 map.\n- **Hardest:** Keeping the demo complete while honoring the 10-foot rules without a real pad or TV.\n- **One test I'd run:** A real DualSense in a browser."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":664,"cache_read_input_tokens":271872,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":664},"output_tokens":742,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5584c513-9280-4b46-aaca-c597f867a104","uuid":"828abf45-4c69-44d2-b282-7edb474b1cb4","timestamp":"2026-11-03T21:02:26.210Z","request_id":"req_011Cggfq9i8nxusuDPFJw86b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The html bundle, exit-interview.json and all four feedback sections are present, and the exit interview parses. But TASK.md's requirement that every feature be implemented is not clearly met. The final reply states the map is 48×34 'smaller than the plan's 72×56', that real controller, TV and iOS Safari testing were not done, and that only a few screens were visually reviewed. TASK.md asks for a complete, polished demo that shows all features working, so these gaps in verification and scope are not closed by the transcript."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5584c513-9280-4b46-aaca-c597f867a104","uuid":"25993173-1b4e-4ea0-b760-a68e8a3900b3","timestamp":"2026-11-03T21:02:30.580Z","isSynthetic":true}
